n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\narXiv:2410.13290v1 [math.CO] 17 Oct 2024\n\nPACKING LARGE BALANCED TREES INTO BIPARTITE GRAPHS\n\nCRISTINA G. FERNANDES, TÁSSIO NAIA, GIOVANNE SANTOS, AND MAYA STEIN\n\nAbstract. We prove that for every γ > 0 there exists n 0∈N such that for every n≥n 0 any\n\nfamily of up to ⌊n 1\n\n2+γ⌋trees having at most (1 −γ)n vertices in each bipartition class can be\n\npacked into K n,n. As a tool for our proof, we show an approximate bipartite version of the\n\nKomlós–Sárközy–Szemerédi Theorem, which we believe to be of independent interest.\n\n§1. Introduction\n\nA family H of graphs packs into a graph G if G contains pairwise edge-disjoint copies of the\n\nmembers of H, i.e. a packing of H. A packing is perfect if it uses each edge of G. A graph H\n\ndecomposes a graph G if there is a perfect packing of copies of H into G.\n\nPacking and decomposition problems have a long history, naturally relating to Euler’s 1782\n\nquestion on the existence of orthogonal latin squares, and to the existence of designs. We are\n\ninterested in packing trees into complete bipartite graphs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSmale's problems (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smale%27s_problems)\nciteturn5search18 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;  16th  | Jacobian conjecture: If F : k^{N} → k^{N} is regular mapping and the Jacobian determinant of F is a non-zero constant and k has characteristic 0, then F has an inverse function G : k^{N} → k^{N} that is also regular. ... \"A theorem on non-tatonnement stability\". ... Journal of the London Mathematical Society. 113 (1) e70416. arXiv:2301.08221. doi:10.1112/jlms.70416. The Jacobian conjecture ... is one of the outstanding open problems in all of mathematics ... even the case n = 2 is st